native-xwidget: a `native' xwidget type whose view a dynamic module supplies.

NS (Cocoa) only.  `make-xwidget' accepts TYPE `native'; ARGUMENTS is then
a user pointer, made by a dynamic module, whose pointer is an NSView the
module created and holds a reference to.  Emacs retains the view for the
widget's life, puts it in the widget's container (xwWindow) as it does a
WKWebView, and releases it in nsxwidget_kill.  Redisplay moves, resizes,
clips and hides the view through the same code as a WebKit view, with one
difference: a native view's per-window container is hidden with its
`hidden' property instead of being moved to (10000, 10000), so AppKit
sends the module's view `viewDidHide' and `viewDidUnhide' and the module
can stop drawing while it is out of sight.  WebKit views keep the move.

The per-window container (XvWindow) now clips to its bounds, for both
types.  Redisplay clips a widget by sizing that container, but AppKit
stopped clipping subviews by default for programs linked against macOS 14
or later, so a widget cut by a window edge drew past it.

The xwidget-webkit-* functions already check the type and signal
"Not a WebKit widget" for a native widget.  nsxwidget_kill skips the
WebKit teardown for it, and returns early for an xwidget that was already
killed: on NS `kill-xwidget' leaves the buffer set, so a second call
reached nsxwidget_kill again and released the container twice (found by
reading the source).  GTK and PGTK are unchanged: there `native' is still
a bad xwidget type.

